Subject: [PATCH] Export host-coordinated POSIX byte-range locks per guest owner

Negotiate FUSE_POSIX_LOCKS with the opt-in --shared-posix-locks flag.
FUSE lock_owner names a guest files_struct; keep one host fd per
(inode, owner) and issue nonblocking F_OFD_GETLK/F_OFD_SETLK on it so
independent guest processes and host processes conflict bidirectionally,
while one owner's opens, dup/fork/exec identity and split/merge semantics
match POSIX. FLUSH (every guest close) drops the owner entry, reproducing
"any close releases the owner's locks"; F_SETLKW rides the existing
cancellable retry queue with EINTR/grant race handling. Fails hard at
INIT instead of silently falling back to guest-local locks.

Wire decoding uses inclusive endpoints (start=0,end=0 is a single byte;
start>0,end=0 is invalid) and OFFSET_MAX = 2^63-1 for to-EOF.

The host fcntl and the per-owner shadow interval mutation are ONE
per-inode critical section, so a same-owner unlock on another thread can
never overwrite a just-granted record (no phantom intervals). The
blocking path performs syscall+shadow atomically itself; callers do not
apply a second note afterward.

A GLOBAL per-table waiter graph reports EDEADLK for guest-owner wait
cycles across any number of files (cross-inode ABBA, not just same-file).
Cycle verification is deterministic: reachability only traverses waiters
whose inode teardown locks are held (same Arc identities, acquired in
ascending Inode order together with the global waiter registry), and the
locked set expands in order until the cycle is either fully frozen
(EDEADLK) or proves absent (EAGAIN); a candidate cycle that changes before
verification cannot produce a false positive. flush/interrupt fail
pending waiters with EINTR without resurrecting the owner, and canceling
one edge cannot manufacture a cycle. A shadow interval set attributes
GETLK conflicts with the guest tgid recorded from fuse_lk_in.lk.pid
(host-process holders report 0). Migration of lock state stays
unsupported.

